## 💡 Introduction

A terminal is an interface that allows users to communicate with the operating system using commands instead of interacting entirely through a graphical interface.

Windows provides two commonly used command-line shells:

- **CMD (Command Prompt):** Traditional Windows command interpreter.
- **PowerShell:** Modern command-line shell and scripting language built on the .NET ecosystem.

Both can execute programs, manage files, and perform system operations. However, PowerShell provides much more advanced capabilities for automation and system administration.

## 📊 CMD vs PowerShell: Quick Comparison

| Feature | CMD | PowerShell |
|---|---|---|
| Introduction | 1980s-era Windows command environment | Introduced in 2006 |
| Executable | `cmd.exe` | `powershell.exe` / `pwsh.exe` |
| Data Handling | Primarily text | Structured objects |
| Scripting | Batch (`.bat`, `.cmd`) | PowerShell (`.ps1`) |
| Automation | Basic to moderate | Advanced |
| File Management | Supported | Advanced |
| Process Management | Text-based commands | Object-based management |
| System Administration | Limited capabilities | Extensive capabilities |
| .NET Integration | No native object pipeline | Native integration |
| Learning Curve | Easier initially | Requires more learning |
| AI/ML Development | Fully usable | More convenient for automation |

