## ⚡ 2. Understanding PowerShell

PowerShell is Microsoft's modern command-line shell and scripting environment.

Unlike CMD, PowerShell is designed for advanced automation, system administration, process management, and working with structured data.

### Basic Architecture

```text
        User
          |
          v
      PowerShell
          |
          v
    PowerShell Engine
          |
          v
    .NET Objects
          |
          v
    Windows APIs / OS
```

PowerShell supports:

- Command execution.
- Advanced scripting.
- Object-based pipelines.
- .NET integration.
- Process and service management.
- Registry management.
- System automation.
- Cloud and enterprise administration.

### Common PowerShell Commands

| Command | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `Get-ChildItem` | List files and folders |
| `Set-Location` | Change directory |
| `New-Item` | Create files and folders |
| `Remove-Item` | Delete files and folders |
| `Copy-Item` | Copy files |
| `Move-Item` | Move files |
| `Get-Process` | Retrieve running processes |
| `Stop-Process` | Terminate processes |
| `Get-Service` | Retrieve Windows services |
| `Restart-Service` | Restart services |
| `Get-ComputerInfo` | Retrieve system information |
| `Get-Command` | Discover available commands |

PowerShell also supports familiar commands such as `dir`, `cd`, and `copy` through aliases.

