## 🔐 6. Administrator vs Normal Mode

This distinction is extremely important.

CMD vs PowerShell determines **which shell you use**.

Administrator vs Normal determines **which permissions the shell receives**.

They are two separate concepts.

### Normal Mode

When you open CMD or PowerShell normally, the shell runs using your standard user permissions.

You can generally:

- Execute Python programs.
- Install packages in your user environment.
- Run Git commands.
- Create project directories.
- Run Ollama.
- Start Jupyter Notebook.
- Develop applications.

### Administrator Mode

When you select **Run as Administrator**, Windows starts the shell with an elevated security token.

This provides permission to perform certain protected system operations.

Examples:

- Modify protected system directories.
- Manage Windows services.
- Install device drivers.
- Modify machine-wide settings.
- Configure firewall rules.
- Modify protected registry locations.

### Internal Difference

```text
NORMAL MODE

User
  |
  v
CMD / PowerShell
  |
  v
Standard User Token
  |
  v
Allowed User Operations
```

```text
ADMINISTRATOR MODE

User
  |
  v
Run as Administrator
  |
  v
CMD / PowerShell
  |
  v
Elevated Security Token
  |
  v
Privileged System Operations
```

**Important:** The shell itself does not become more powerful simply because it is running as Administrator. It receives additional Windows permissions.

