## ⚙️ 7. When Do You Need Administrator Permissions?

| Operation | Normal Mode | Administrator |
|---|---|---|
| Run Python scripts | ✅ | Not needed |
| Install user-level packages | ✅ | Not needed |
| Git clone / pull | ✅ | Not needed |
| Run Ollama models | ✅ | Not needed |
| Run Jupyter Notebook | ✅ | Not needed |
| Create project folders | ✅ | Not needed |
| Install NVIDIA drivers | ❌ | Usually required |
| Install CUDA system components | ❌ | Usually required |
| Configure WSL features | Limited | Usually required |
| Install Docker Desktop | Limited | Often required during setup |
| Modify firewall rules | ❌ | Usually required |
| Manage protected services | Limited | Usually required |
| Modify machine-wide environment variables | ❌ | Usually required |

### Environment Variables Example

PowerShell:

**User-level variable:**

```powershell
[Environment]::SetEnvironmentVariable(
    "MY_VARIABLE",
    "MyValue",
    "User"
)
```

**Machine-level variable:**

```powershell
[Environment]::SetEnvironmentVariable(
    "MY_VARIABLE",
    "MyValue",
    "Machine"
)
```

Machine-level modifications generally require Administrator permissions.

### Common Beginner Mistake

Running every command as Administrator is unnecessary and potentially risky.

Why avoid it?

1. Unnecessary access to protected system resources.
2. Increased consequences if a script contains mistakes.
3. Files created by elevated processes can have different ownership and permissions.
4. Permission problems may remain hidden during development.

### Recommended Approach

```text
Open Normal Terminal
        |
        v
Execute Command
        |
        v
Does It Require Elevated Permissions?
        |
    +---+---+
    |       |
   NO      YES
    |       |
    v       v
Continue   Run as
Normally   Administrator
```

