## 🤖 8. Practical Applications in AI/LLM Engineering

Now, why does understanding CMD and PowerShell actually matter when developing AI applications?

When working with modern AI systems, you frequently interact with:

- Python environments
- Hugging Face Transformers
- PyTorch
- TensorFlow
- Ollama
- LangChain
- LangGraph
- FastAPI
- Docker
- CUDA
- Git and GitHub
- Local LLM inference servers

Most of these tools do not specifically require PowerShell. They can work through CMD as well.

However, PowerShell becomes particularly useful when managing and automating the surrounding development environment.

### Real-World Examples

| LLM Engineering Task | Useful PowerShell Command |
|---|---|
| Activate virtual environment | `.\venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1` |
| Install dependencies | `pip install -r requirements.txt` |
| Run FastAPI | `uvicorn main:app --reload` |
| Find Python processes | `Get-Process python` |
| Stop stuck Python processes | `Stop-Process -Name python` |
| Find configuration files | `Get-ChildItem *.env -Recurse` |
| Monitor Ollama | `Get-Process ollama` |
| Inspect system services | `Get-Service` |
| Check GPU information | `Get-CimInstance Win32_VideoController` |
| Start Docker containers | `docker compose up -d` |
| Inspect environment variables | `Get-ChildItem Env:` |

### Example: Starting an LLM Project

```powershell
# Create project directory
mkdir llm-project

# Enter project directory
cd llm-project

# Create virtual environment
python -m venv venv

# Activate virtual environment
.\venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1

# Install dependencies
pip install fastapi uvicorn langchain

# Create application file
New-Item main.py -ItemType File

# Run application
uvicorn main:app --reload
```

### Example: Managing Local LLM Processes

```powershell
# Find Ollama processes
Get-Process ollama

# Check whether Ollama service exists
Get-Service *ollama*

# Display GPU information
Get-CimInstance Win32_VideoController

# Display Python processes
Get-Process python
```

These operations become useful when developing local AI agents, RAG applications, multimodal systems, and LLM-powered APIs.

